A||||13||pHoOl||Carbon in tailpipe||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||05-27-2001||08:40 PM||||I've been thinking about polishing up the pipes, and when i went to clean them, there was a bunch of carbon all over the inside of them.  <P>I've been told that this could be that the engine is running rich,  but with my limited car knowledge, i don't have the slightest clue how to adjust this on a fuel injected car.  I suppose that i could take the cat. off and put some sparkplugs in the tailpipes, but i don't think that burning cars that are riding my arse would solve the problem.  (though it would be a fun fix)<P>Anybody have a solution as to how to check if it's running lean, and how to fix it?<P>Thanks much,<BR>matt<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||05-27-2001||09:07 PM||||one question first, when was the last time you changed your O2 sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000002||pHoOl||05-27-2001||10:15 PM||||Well, i've had the car for over a year, and haven't changed it.  However, i'm not getting any engine codes.  I did just replace a EGR valve gasket cause it was blown.  but my dad and i thought that would make it run lean if anything.  I have noticed that the car runs a lot quieter now that i dont' have an exhaust leak.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||05-27-2001||11:02 PM||||try changing the O2 sensor first - you should change it yearly - that will cause it to run rich and you won't get a code for it.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||05-27-2001||11:19 PM||||Why is it that you won't get a code for it?  If it's faulty, there is a code that tells if it's running rich, and another to tell if it's running lean.  Sorry, just quoting what my father the engineer will say.  But seriously... is it because it is within spec of the range, just not out of whack enough to trip the codes?<P>How much are O2 sensors?<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||05-28-2001||12:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>try changing the O2 sensor first - you should change it yearly - that will cause it to run rich and you won't get a code for it.....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm curious about this myself. I've had my car over a year and haven't changed the sensor. I figured I'd get a code too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||05-28-2001||12:19 AM||||it is possible that you may get a code (13) but not always. It is just a suggestion as it will affect the hydrocarbons if the O2 is bad. I change mune every year just as I do plugs, etc. I make it a normal part of the tune-up routine.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sjp777||05-28-2001||12:20 AM||||It won't give a code because the ecm doesn't think there is anything really wrong. It gets an reduced output voltage from the  worn out sensor which tells it the car is running a little lean, so to compensate it richens up the fuel mixture. Now the car is actually running rich, but the weakened signal from the O2 sensor shows normal to the ecm.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bruce||05-28-2001||01:54 PM||||How difficult is it to change the O2 sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||05-28-2001||02:25 PM||||not difficult at all from under the car. I use an O2 sensor socket which is a deep socket with a slot in it for the wire. Auto parts stores have them. Otherwize, just wrench it off and on......you might want to spray penetrating oil on the sensor where it mounts to the y-pipe first to make removal easier....||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||10-02-2001||03:48 AM||||My GT is running very rich. As it is sitting there idling it will splatter little fuel spots on your hand, and leave black soot on the rear fascia. <P>I looked in the Haynes manual for the location of the O2 sensor on my V6. Found it the first time. <BR>Cracked it loose with a Crescent. Was able to loosen it by hand. Haynes manual recommends warming the engine to at least 120 degrees before attempting this. I did, and burned myself a little despite the caution about this.<P>I'm gonna replace it tomorrow, and hopefully along with the new EGR it should clean it up some.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hank is Here||10-02-2001||09:31 AM||||Oh they cost about $20 for bosch or about $45 for delco at advance auto||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackARROW||10-02-2001||04:53 PM||||Rich means what?  Too much gas, not enough air?<P>I wonder, I put 91 Octane gas in it (feeling it runs better).  Would that make my motor run rich?<P>Running rich, would that worn out the spark plugs quicker?  <P>And what would be the effect on performance?<P>Enough questions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>BlackARROW &gt;&gt;--&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85<BR>"Get your groove on"||||reg||1
Z||000013||pHoOl||10-03-2001||12:54 AM||||I have heard that the best thing that you can do is run it at the octane that it was designed to run at.  From what i'm told, premium can even hinder performance.<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
